DOGE-79 — Gaza Redevelopment Planning, Digital Land Tokens, Terrorist-Finance Exposure, DOGE-Related Oversight Loss, and the Alleged Suppression of a Trump-Branded Commercialization Strategy

I. Investigative proposition

This section examines the documented convergence of five developments:

the destruction and mass displacement of Gaza’s population;
President Donald Trump’s proposal for the United States to assume control of Gaza and redevelop it as the “Riviera of the Middle East”;
privately developed reconstruction proposals contemplating digital tokens for Palestinian land;
consulting and financial modeling concerning population relocation, infrastructure, tourism, technology zones, and investor returns; and
DOGE-related reductions that impaired federal humanitarian-assistance oversight and counter-diversion controls.

The attached SECTION TRUMP TOWER GAZA — PREMEDITATED ETHNIC CLEANSING identifies this convergence as evidence of a premeditated commercial-displacement scheme and describes the contemplated luxury development as “Trump Tower Gaza.”

The public record verifies a Trump-branded Gaza redevelopment vision, the GREAT Trust proposal, digital land-token provisions, relocation modeling, proposed luxury resorts and technology zones, and disruption of federal aid oversight. It also verifies that terrorist organizations and their supporters have used cryptocurrency fundraising and transfer networks.

The presently located record does not independently establish:

a finalized Trump Organization contract to construct a building formally named “Trump Tower Gaza”;
a registered Trump Tower Gaza project company;
construction permits, architectural approvals, financing closings, or commencement of such a tower;
that digital assets financed the destruction of Gaza for redevelopment purposes;
that DOGE concealed a particular Trump Tower contract; or
that the Gaza redevelopment proposals were the original reason for the October 2023 attack or the subsequent military campaign.

Those propositions require additional records. They should be investigated aggressively but should not be collapsed into the facts already established.

II. The documented Trump Gaza redevelopment position

On February 4, 2025, President Trump publicly stated that the United States would “take over” the Gaza Strip, assume responsibility for clearing and redeveloping it, and create economic development and jobs. He described Gaza as potentially becoming the “Riviera of the Middle East.”

These were presidential statements made alongside Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, not merely commentary by an unaffiliated developer. Official presidential remarks

Later that month, Trump published an artificial-intelligence-generated “Trump Gaza” video portraying Gaza as a luxury destination. The imagery included Trump-branded resort features, money, yachts, a golden Trump statue, and Elon Musk.

That video was not a construction contract or architectural filing. It was nevertheless a presidentially distributed representation linking the destruction of Gaza to a Trump-branded luxury-development vision. Washington Post report on the “Trump Gaza” video

Accordingly, the existence of a Trump-branded Gaza commercialization concept is documented. The narrower claim that an actual Trump Tower Gaza development was contractually authorized or placed into construction must be proven through project records.

III. The GREAT Trust development proposal

A 38-page proposal titled the Gaza Reconstitution, Economic Acceleration and Transformation Trust, or GREAT Trust, subsequently circulated within Trump-administration circles.

The proposal contemplated:

United States administration of Gaza through a trusteeship lasting at least ten years;

temporary or permanent relocation of Gaza’s population;

restricted residential zones during reconstruction;
six to eight “AI-powered” smart cities;
luxury coastal development;
logistics, port, railway, airport, manufacturing, energy, and technology projects;
an “Elon Musk Smart Manufacturing Zone”;
public-land monetization;
private-investor participation; and
anticipated returns derived from redevelopment.
The underlying prospectus is publicly available through the Washington Post’s document repository. GREAT Trust prospectus
The proposal reportedly offered Palestinian landowners a digital token in exchange for redevelopment rights, which could be applied toward relocation elsewhere or later redeemed for housing in a newly developed Gaza. Washington Post investigation

This is not a speculative connection invented after the fact. Tokenization, population relocation, land control, private development, and investor returns appeared together in the documented proposal.

The remaining question is whether the prospectus was merely an unsolicited planning document or became a basis for government policy, contracting, military planning, humanitarian operations, or private investment.

IV. “Trump Tower Gaza” versus the documented record

For evidentiary precision, investigators should distinguish three different propositions:

PropositionEvidentiary statusTrump publicly promoted U.S. control and luxury redevelopment of GazaDocumentedTrump distributed a “Trump Gaza” video containing Trump-branded resort imageryDocumentedThe GREAT Trust proposed tokenized land rights, relocation, luxury development and private megaprojectsDocumentedA Trump Organization entity entered a finalized contract for “Trump Tower Gaza”Not established by the presently identified public recordConstruction of a Trump Tower in Gaza commencedNot established by the presently identified public record

The phrase “Trump Tower Gaza” may accurately describe the alleged Trump-branded real-estate component under investigation. It should not be represented as a completed or legally approved building without:

a development agreement;
land-control instrument;
corporate filing;
Trump trademark or licensing agreement;
architectural plan identifying the tower;
financing agreement;
government authorization;
construction tender; or
site activity.
Reporting in August 2026 described the Gaza reconstruction authority’s first contemplated construction contract as a military outpost rather than a Trump-branded tower, and stated that broader awards remained unfinished. Guardian report on the first reported contract
This does not disprove undisclosed planning. It means the project’s exact contractual status remains a central discovery question.
V. Project Aurora and relocation modeling
The attached exhibit connects “Trump Tower Gaza” to an initiative described as Project Aurora.
Public reporting substantiates significant portions of the broader Aurora account. Boston Consulting Group personnel reportedly participated in financial modeling involving the possible relocation of Palestinians and work associated with Gaza humanitarian and redevelopment planning.
BCG itself acknowledged that:

a United States team began pro bono Gaza-related work in October 2024;

two partners failed to disclose the full nature of that work;
subsequent work was unauthorized;
the individuals committed serious failures of judgment;
the partners were removed; and
BCG disavowed and stopped the work.
BCG maintains that the work was secretly conducted outside authorized company scope and that BCG received no payment. BCG statement
That statement is important corroboration that Gaza planning occurred inside a major consulting organization and that its nature produced internal disciplinary consequences. It is also counterevidence against attributing the work automatically to BCG’s authorized corporate leadership.
Investigators must obtain the complete Aurora files rather than treating either the leaked documents or BCG’s corporate disavowal as the final account.
VI. Digital land tokens as instruments of dispossession risk

The proposed digital land token was not merely a new payment technology. It potentially would have converted a Palestinian owner’s real-property claim into a programmable contractual instrument administered by a reconstruction trust.

That structure creates fundamental questions:

What exact property right would the owner surrender?

Would acceptance permanently waive title, inheritance, return, occupancy, or compensation claims?

Who would issue and control the token?

What law would govern it?

Would a Palestinian court have jurisdiction?

Could the token expire, be frozen, transferred, pledged, or cancelled?
What reserves or assets would support redemption?
Would redemption depend on completing relocation?
Could purchasers acquire the tokens at distressed prices?
Would sanctions, identity systems, or smart-contract conditions restrict use?
Who would control the land after token issuance?

Would recipients receive informed, voluntary consent while displaced and dependent on aid?

A token offered to a displaced family under conditions of hunger, confinement, or homelessness cannot be assumed to reflect ordinary market consent.

The concern is therefore not that blockchain technology automatically causes ethnic cleansing. The concern is that tokenization could provide a technical and legal mechanism for converting contested land rights into transferable instruments while separating dispossessed owners from the underlying property.

VII. Digital assets and terrorism financing

The broader statement that unregulated digital assets create terrorism-financing channels is supported, but it requires qualification.

Digital assets are not universally unregulated. United States exchanges and money-services businesses may be subject to Bank Secrecy Act, sanctions, customer-identification, suspicious-activity-reporting, and state-licensing requirements.

However, global regulation and enforcement remain incomplete. Risk increases through:

offshore exchanges;
unregistered brokers;
peer-to-peer transfers;
self-hosted wallets;
stablecoins;
cross-chain bridges;
mixers and obfuscation services;
nominee accounts;
fraudulent identification;
decentralized services;
rapid wallet replacement; and
jurisdictions that have not implemented FATF standards.

FATF’s 2025 review concluded that global implementation of virtual-asset safeguards remained insufficient and identified continuing illicit-finance risks, including increased misuse of stablecoins. FATF 2025 targeted update

Treasury has sanctioned Hamas-aligned fundraising networks that solicited or facilitated virtual-currency transfers. Treasury sanctions announcement

In March 2025, the Justice Department announced a seizure action involving more than $200,000 in cryptocurrency connected to an alleged Hamas fundraising and laundering operation. Justice Department seizure announcement

Accordingly, virtual assets are a documented modern terrorism-financing method. But the evidence does not support describing them as the exclusive or necessarily dominant method. Terrorist financing also continues through cash, informal value-transfer systems, charities, commercial businesses, banks, commodities, and state support.

VIII. The two distinct digital-asset channels

DOGE-79 must avoid conflating two separate channels.

A. Terrorist-funding channel

This involves digital assets used to:
solicit donations;
move value across borders;
evade sanctions;
obscure intermediaries;
fragment transactions;
replace exposed wallet addresses; or
convert funds through exchanges and brokers.
B. Redevelopment and land-tokenization channel
This involves digital instruments used to:
obtain redevelopment rights;
represent compensation;

monetize land;

attract investors;

create special-purpose vehicles;

distribute project returns;

replace conventional title claims; or

condition compensation upon relocation.

Evidence that cryptocurrency was used for terrorist fundraising does not itself prove that the GREAT Trust’s proposed land tokens were part of terrorist financing.

The potential connection must be established through common wallets, issuers, beneficial owners, exchanges, financial sponsors, developers, or transactional flows.

IX. How an underregulated channel can finance destabilization

A digital-asset system can facilitate destabilization when it allows different actors to finance different stages of a conflict economy without transparent beneficial ownership.
Potential stages include:
financing armed organizations or extremist fundraising;
sanctions evasion and procurement of prohibited goods;
private-security and logistics payments;
acquisition of distressed claims or property interests;
tokenization of reconstruction or land rights;

speculative investment in post-conflict projects;

laundering proceeds through exchanges or stablecoins; and

returning profits through offshore companies or investment funds.
That is a valid investigative model. It becomes a proven integrated scheme only when financial records connect the actors and transactions across those stages.
The essential evidence is not the shared use of the word “token.” It is the movement of identifiable value among identifiable participants.
X. DOGE-related loss of humanitarian oversight
DOGE’s relevance is clearest in the documented disruption of USAID and its oversight capacity.
In February 2025, the USAID Office of Inspector General reported that the foreign-assistance pause, stop-work instructions, and widespread staffing reductions had:
degraded USAID’s ability to distribute and safeguard humanitarian assistance;
made oversight functions largely nonoperational;

impaired partner vetting;

weakened monitoring intended to prevent diversion to designated terrorist organizations; and

placed hundreds of millions of dollars in food assistance at risk of spoilage, storage costs, or diversion.

The report identified approximately $8.2 billion in obligated but undisbursed humanitarian assistance then subject to substantially impaired oversight. USAID OIG alert

This creates a profound contradiction:

An initiative presented as preventing government fraud reduced the government’s capacity to determine whether humanitarian resources were diverted to sanctioned or violent actors.

The Trump administration removed USAID Inspector General Paul Martin shortly after the warning was issued. The timing warrants scrutiny, although temporal proximity alone does not prove retaliatory intent or a Gaza-specific cover-up.
XI. The alleged suppression mechanism
The attached exhibit asserts that the public record was suppressed. That allegation should be tested through identifiable acts rather than inferred merely from incomplete reporting.
Possible suppression mechanisms include:
classifying redevelopment planning as humanitarian or security work;
using private consultants outside ordinary contracting channels;
excluding plans from federal recordkeeping systems;
using personal email or ephemeral messaging;

withholding beneficial owners of project entities;

omitting digital-token issuers and administrators;

describing relocation modeling as neutral economic analysis;

removing auditors or investigators;

terminating personnel with access to relevant records;

preventing inspector-general review;

allowing contractor data to be deleted;
denying Freedom of Information Act requests through overbroad exemptions; or
making public claims inconsistent with internal plans.
Proof requires the underlying communication, preservation notice, deletion event, misleading statement, withheld record, or interference with a specific inquiry.
An incomplete public record is not by itself proof that the record was intentionally suppressed.

XII. International-law implications

The International Court of Justice has issued provisional measures in the proceeding brought by South Africa under the Genocide Convention. Provisional measures are legally significant but are not a final merits judgment that genocide occurred. ICJ case materials

In September 2025, the United Nations Independent International Commission of Inquiry concluded that Israeli authorities and forces had committed four genocidal acts in Gaza and found genocidal intent. That is a formal UN investigative conclusion; it is distinct from a final judgment by the ICJ or a criminal conviction of any individual. UN Commission report

Relevant legal questions surrounding redevelopment include:

whether displacement was voluntary or coerced;

whether displaced residents retained an effective right to return;

whether property transfers occurred under duress;

whether an occupying power permanently altered or exploited occupied territory;

whether private actors knowingly facilitated unlawful transfer;
whether reconstruction plans were contingent upon permanent population removal; and
whether commercial actors knowingly profited from internationally wrongful conduct.
A redevelopment proposal prepared after widespread destruction is not, by itself, proof that its authors caused the destruction. Premeditation would require evidence that relevant actors planned, financed, encouraged, or facilitated the destructive conduct before or while it occurred for the purpose of enabling redevelopment.
XIII. RICO analysis
The following acts do not independently establish racketeering:
proposing a reconstruction plan;
investing in postwar infrastructure;
using blockchain technology;
preparing an economic model;
publishing a Trump-branded video;
advising a humanitarian organization; or
advocating a controversial relocation policy.

RICO relevance would require an enterprise, participation in its affairs, a pattern of qualifying predicate offenses, and—where civil relief is sought—proximate injury to business or property.

Potential predicates could arise if evidence establishes:

wire or mail fraud through false representations about land rights, token value, consent, project authorization, or beneficial ownership;

material support for designated terrorist organizations;

sanctions evasion;

money laundering;

bribery connected to project approvals or public contracts;

obstruction through destruction or concealment of planning records;
witness retaliation;
theft or conversion of property;
fraudulent token sales; or
interstate transportation of criminal proceeds.
International-law violations are not automatically RICO predicates. The analysis must connect each alleged act to a qualifying offense listed in 18 U.S.C. § 1961.
XIV. Evidence required

Investigators should obtain:

every version of the GREAT Trust prospectus;

complete Aurora project files;

metadata identifying document authors and revision histories;

Trump Organization trademark, licensing and development records concerning Gaza;
land maps and proposed project coordinates;
communications involving Trump, Jared Kushner, Steve Witkoff, administration officials, Israeli officials, GHF, BCG personnel and prospective investors;
governmental reviews or presentations of the GREAT Trust proposal;
contracts and contemplated awards issued by any Gaza reconstruction authority;
project-company and special-purpose-vehicle records;
beneficial-ownership information;

capitalization tables and investor presentations;

token white papers, smart contracts and reserve arrangements;

wallet addresses and blockchain-analytics reports;
exchange, stablecoin and custodian records;
sanctions-screening and suspicious-activity records;
models assigning values to Palestinian land;
consent, waiver and relocation forms;
population-transfer cost models;
military and reconstruction maps;
USAID and State Department Gaza-assistance records;
DOGE communications involving USAID, humanitarian oversight or Gaza;
USAID OIG preservation and investigative files;

inspector-general termination communications;

deleted-message and device-forensic evidence;

foreign-agent and lobbying disclosures; and

evidence showing when each participant first learned of the redevelopment proposals.

XV. Counterevidence and limitations
A defensible report must retain the following:
No presently identified public contract proves construction of a formal Trump Tower Gaza.
The GREAT Trust was a proposal, and circulation within government circles does not alone prove government adoption.
Companies named in private presentations may not have agreed to participate. Reporting indicates that several companies whose names or logos appeared in the plans denied involvement.
BCG states that the work was unauthorized, hidden from leadership and never approved as a BCG project.
Digital assets have legitimate uses and are subject to regulation in many jurisdictions.
Documented terrorist use of cryptocurrency does not prove that every Gaza-related token proposal involved terrorism financing.
DOGE-related damage to USAID oversight does not alone prove that DOGE intended to facilitate Gaza redevelopment or suppress a tower project.
A post-destruction development proposal does not establish that the destruction was initiated for that development.
Criminal intent must be proven separately for each person and entity.
XVI. Preliminary finding
Status: major land-rights, illicit-finance, humanitarian-oversight and obstruction investigation warranted; an integrated premeditated Trump Tower financing conspiracy is not yet established by the located record.
The public record establishes substantially more than an abstract real-estate fantasy.
President Trump publicly proposed American control and luxury redevelopment of Gaza. He circulated Trump-branded resort imagery. A detailed GREAT Trust prospectus contemplated the relocation of Gaza’s population, the exchange of Palestinian redevelopment rights for digital tokens, AI-powered cities, luxury development, private investment and anticipated financial returns. Consulting personnel modeled related relocation and redevelopment issues, and BCG later acknowledged unauthorized Gaza work serious enough to produce dismissals and a corporate disavowal.
The public record also establishes that cryptocurrency has been used in Hamas-related fundraising and laundering operations and that global virtual-asset safeguards remain incomplete.
At the same time, DOGE-related staffing and operational reductions impaired USAID’s ability to monitor humanitarian funds, screen partners, prevent diversion and preserve institutional oversight.
Together, these facts justify investigation of whether underregulated digital-asset channels were used to finance destabilization, acquire or tokenize displaced people’s property, conceal beneficial ownership, or distribute redevelopment profits—and whether DOGE-related institutional disruption concealed or facilitated any part of that process.
The evidence presently supports a documented Trump Gaza redevelopment and digital-land-token plan. The more specific claim of a finalized or constructed Trump Tower Gaza, and the claim that destruction was originally undertaken to enable that project, remain propositions requiring contracts, financial transfers, contemporaneous communications and proof of intent.
