DOGE-80 — Project Aurora, Humanitarian Transition Areas, Private Security Contractors, Biometric Aid Controls, and the Alleged Conversion of Humanitarian Relief into Displacement Infrastructure

I. Investigative proposition

This section examines whether humanitarian relief in Gaza was deliberately restructured from a decentralized civilian-aid system into a privately operated, militarized, data-driven mechanism capable of:

concentrating civilians at a small number of controlled distribution sites;
compelling population movement through dependence on food and medicine;
identifying, screening, surveilling, and categorizing aid recipients;
replacing United Nations agencies and established humanitarian organizations with private contractors;
creating “humanitarian transition areas” in which displaced Palestinians could be confined or processed;
generating demographic, biometric, logistical, and property information useful for relocation and redevelopment;
supporting Project Aurora’s financial modeling of Palestinian departure from Gaza; and
preparing territory for the Trump administration’s proposed postwar redevelopment plans.

The public record establishes the existence of Project Aurora-related relocation modeling, the Gaza Humanitarian Foundation’s contractor-operated distribution system, armed private-security participation, reported facial-recognition screening, a highly concentrated network of distribution hubs, and the discussion of “humanitarian transition areas.”

It does not yet establish that every element operated as a unified criminal plan, that biometric information was transferred into a redevelopment or population-removal database, or that DOGE personnel directly designed the Gaza Humanitarian Foundation system.

II. Project Aurora

Project Aurora reportedly originated as work connected to the creation and operation of the Gaza Humanitarian Foundation, or GHF.

According to public reporting and Boston Consulting Group’s subsequent admissions, BCG personnel began Gaza-related work in October 2024. The work later expanded beyond ordinary humanitarian logistics into financial modeling addressing Gaza’s postwar future and the possible relocation of Palestinians.

Reported Project Aurora models examined:

relocation of as many as 500,000 Palestinians;

direct payments estimated at approximately $9,000 per person;
alternative packages combining cash, housing, and food support;
the comparative cost of supporting Palestinians inside Gaza versus financing departure;
reconstruction-period population movements;
humanitarian logistics;
temporary residential or transition arrangements; and
potential economic benefits attributed to relocation.
A financial model is not automatically an adopted government policy. But modeling the price of removing a civilian population can make displacement appear to be a neutral budgetary choice rather than a question of protected rights, consent, return, property, and survival.

BCG states that the relevant work was unauthorized, secretly continued by two partners, inconsistent with company instructions, and never approved as a BCG project. BCG dismissed the partners, disavowed the work, and stated that the company received no payment. BCG statement

That explanation is important counterevidence concerning institutional authorization. It does not eliminate the need to determine who requested, received, financed, reviewed, or acted upon the resulting models.

III. Humanitarian relief as infrastructure

Humanitarian aid normally serves a protective function: relief should reach civilians based on need and should not be conditioned on political allegiance, compelled movement, surrender of property, intelligence cooperation, or participation in a redevelopment program.

Aid infrastructure can acquire a second function when the authority controlling food also controls:

the locations where food may be collected;

the routes civilians must travel;

the hours during which movement is permitted;
security screening;
recipient identification;
the information collected about families;
eligibility determinations;
the size and frequency of distributions;
access to medical or shelter services;
the ability to remain in a particular area; and
the consequences of refusing relocation.
Under those conditions, humanitarian logistics can become population-management infrastructure.
The decisive question is whether the GHF system delivered emergency relief while preserving civilian autonomy or used emergency dependence to reorganize where civilians lived, traveled, registered, and ultimately could return.

IV. Replacement of the established aid network

The Gaza Humanitarian Foundation presented itself as an alternative to the United Nations-centered aid-distribution network.

Its proposed system concentrated distribution through a small number of secured sites rather than the much broader network of warehouses, community facilities, bakeries, medical organizations, local partners, and relief points traditionally used by humanitarian agencies.

The United Nations and established humanitarian organizations objected that the system:

departed from neutrality, impartiality, independence, and needs-based distribution;

operated under conditions determined by a party to the conflict;

placed distribution facilities in militarized areas;

required civilians to travel long distances through dangerous territory;
excluded vulnerable people unable to make those journeys;
risked forcing civilians toward particular areas;
exposed recipients to surveillance and security screening; and
could not meet the needs of Gaza’s population at the contemplated scale.
The UN proposed an alternative plan using established humanitarian organizations and approximately 400 distribution points. OCHA’s UN-coordinated distribution plan
The choice was therefore not simply “GHF or no aid.” It was also a choice between a broad civilian relief network and a centralized contractor-security model.
V. The private-contractor structure

The GHF system relied upon a layered network of nonprofit, for-profit, private-equity, consulting, logistics, intelligence, and security relationships.

Public reporting identified major operational actors including:

Gaza Humanitarian Foundation, the nominal humanitarian organization;

Safe Reach Solutions, the principal logistics and operational contractor;

UG Solutions, a United States private-security subcontractor;

McNally Capital, which acknowledged an economic interest in Safe Reach Solutions;

Orbis Operations, associated through personnel and private-equity relationships;
former military, intelligence, and government personnel;
Boston Consulting Group personnel involved in early planning; and
Israeli military and government authorities controlling access and security conditions.
Safe Reach Solutions was reportedly organized in Wyoming and placed into a trust administered by Two Ocean Trust. Public corporate records did not transparently disclose the complete economic beneficiaries, funding sources, contractual rights, or distribution of profits.
The Washington Post reported that McNally Capital denied investing in or actively managing Safe Reach Solutions but acknowledged an “economic interest” in the company. Washington Post investigation of the contractor network
This structure requires examination because an entity performing a sovereign-like population-control function should not operate through ownership arrangements that obscure who receives the financial benefit.
VI. Armed security at food-distribution sites

UG Solutions supplied American security contractors for GHF operations. Safe Reach Solutions handled logistics and site operations.

The distribution facilities incorporated:

armed contractor positions;

fencing and controlled lanes;

limited entry and exit points;

surveillance;

crowd-control procedures;

military coordination;
restricted operating periods; and
Israeli forces positioned in or around the surrounding security environment.
Private security is not inherently improper in a conflict zone. Humanitarian convoys and personnel may require protection.
The concern arises when armed personnel become part of the process for determining which civilians may approach food, where they must travel, how they are screened, and what force may be used against crowds composed substantially of hungry and displaced people.
Private contractors ordinarily possess neither the democratic accountability of public institutions nor the recognized neutrality of experienced humanitarian organizations. Their authority, use-of-force standards, immunity, reporting obligations, and evidentiary-preservation requirements must therefore be identified precisely.
VII. Deaths and injuries near distribution routes and sites
The GHF distribution system became associated with repeated reports of civilians being killed or injured while attempting to obtain food.

On June 1, 2025, the UN Human Rights Office reported that the Israeli military killed 32 Palestinians seeking food and described the system as a “militarised humanitarian mechanism.” OHCHR statement

Subsequent UN reporting documented continuing deaths and injuries among aid seekers. The precise attribution of particular incidents remains disputed. GHF denied that lethal incidents occurred inside its distribution sites, while Israeli authorities disputed or questioned accounts of indiscriminate fire. Other reporting, witnesses, medical personnel, UN officials, and some contractor accounts described gunfire and dangerous crowd-control practices on or near routes serving the facilities.

Each incident must be reconstructed separately using:

site video;

contractor body-camera footage;

weapons-discharge records;

radio communications;

military logs;

satellite imagery;

hospital records;
geolocation evidence;
autopsy or projectile evidence; and
witness testimony.
The legal inquiry cannot treat every casualty near a GHF site as automatically attributable to the same actor. It also cannot accept the physical boundary of a distribution compound as a complete defense if civilians were required to pass through predictably lethal surrounding corridors to obtain food.
VIII. Geographic concentration and compelled movement
GHF operated only a few principal distribution locations for a population of more than two million people. Most were situated in southern Gaza or areas under extensive Israeli military control.
This geographic concentration had foreseeable consequences:
northern residents faced longer travel;
elderly, disabled, sick, injured, and unaccompanied people could be excluded in practice;

families had to leave shelters or temporary residences;

civilians accumulated along predictable routes at predictable times;

food access became associated with movement toward specific secured locations;

security authorities could observe and regulate those movements; and

continued residence far from the distribution sites became increasingly difficult.

Aid does not become “voluntary relocation” merely because civilians technically retain the choice to remain hungry.
Investigators must determine whether site placement was based exclusively on security and logistical feasibility or was intended to draw the population toward areas selected for confinement, clearance, emigration, or redevelopment.
IX. Humanitarian transition areas
Parallel planning reportedly considered establishing humanitarian transition areas, or HTAs, designed to house displaced Palestinians in secured locations.
Depending on the version examined, contemplated features reportedly included:
controlled entry and exit;
organized shelter and food distribution;

medical facilities;

biometric or identity screening;

security vetting;

surveillance systems;

separation of persons classified as security risks;

centralized population records;

vocational or administrative services; and
possible preparation for movement elsewhere.
A protected civilian shelter is legally and operationally different from a detention, filtration, or population-transfer facility. Its character depends on actual conditions:
Could civilians leave freely?
Could they return to their homes?
Were families separated?
Was aid available outside the area?
Who controlled the perimeter?
What information was collected?
Were residents asked to waive land or return rights?

Were residents offered relocation packages?

Did refusal affect access to food or protection?
Was the area temporary in fact, or designed for long-term confinement?
The name “humanitarian transition area” cannot determine the legal nature of the facility.
X. Reported biometric and facial-recognition controls
Contemporaneous reporting described facial-recognition or identity-screening measures intended to identify recipients and exclude individuals considered affiliated with Hamas.
A biometric system may be presented as a means of:
preventing duplicate distributions;
reducing diversion;
verifying family eligibility;
identifying sanctioned persons;

protecting staff;

locating missing people; or

improving distribution efficiency.

The same system can create a persistent population-intelligence database containing:

facial images;

identity documents;
family relationships;
residence and displacement history;
aid-collection history;
location and movement patterns;
security classifications;
political or organizational allegations;

medical or disability information; and

links between individuals, households, properties, and devices.
The complete GHF biometric architecture has not been publicly disclosed. Investigators must determine whether facial recognition was operational, tested only in limited form, proposed but never implemented, or performed through an existing Israeli-controlled system.
XI. Biometric accuracy and due-process risks
Biometric identification is probabilistic. Errors can arise through:
low-quality images;
injuries and physical changes;
aging;
dust, masks, head coverings, or poor lighting;
incomplete enrollment data;
duplicate or fraudulent records;

biased or unsuitable training data;

incorrect watchlists;

mistaken family associations; and

human interpretation of uncertain matches.

In an ordinary commercial system, a false match may delay a transaction. In a militarized aid system, a false match may result in:
denial of food;
detention;
interrogation;
family exclusion;
referral to armed authorities;
movement restrictions; or
lethal danger.
Investigators should identify the matching threshold, false-positive rate, watchlist sources, appeal process, human-review requirements, vendor, algorithm version, and every adverse action based upon a biometric result.
No person should be deprived of essential humanitarian relief solely because a private or military algorithm produced an unexplained match.

XII. From aid records to relocation intelligence

Aid-registration information could be combined with other datasets to generate a comprehensive population-management system.
Potential linkages include:
Aid-system informationPossible secondary useIdentity and family recordsHousehold mapping and relationship analysisFacial imagesPersistent identification and surveillanceDistribution attendanceMovement and location historyResidence informationProperty and redevelopment mappingFamily size and vulnerabilityRelocation-cost calculationsDevice or contact informationCommunications and network analysisSecurity classificationsExclusion, detention or targetingAid dependencePrediction of relocation pressureProperty claimsDigital-token or compensation offers
There is no adequate public evidence that all these linkages occurred. Their technical possibility, however, makes the data-flow inquiry essential.
The government must determine whether humanitarian records were transferred to:
Israeli military or intelligence systems;
United States agencies;

private-security companies;

consulting firms;

cloud providers;

redevelopment planners;

token issuers;

prospective investors; or
entities participating in population-relocation planning.
XIII. Aid dependence and coerced consent
Consent obtained during extreme deprivation must be evaluated in context.
A person confronting starvation, bombardment, homelessness, family separation, or denial of medical care may “agree” to:
enter a controlled camp;
provide biometric information;
surrender documents;
accept relocation;
exchange property rights for a token;
move to another country; or
waive a right of return.
The existence of a signature, biometric enrollment, digital acceptance, or token transaction does not by itself establish free and informed consent.
Investigators must determine whether essential assistance was made conditional upon accepting surveillance, relocation, property-transfer, or residency terms.
Humanitarian relief must not become the consideration offered for surrender of protected rights.
XIV. Connection to the GREAT Trust redevelopment proposal
DOGE-79 documented that the GREAT Trust proposal contemplated:
at least ten years of United States administration;
temporary or permanent relocation of Gaza’s population;
digital tokens offered for redevelopment rights;
new AI-powered cities;
luxury resorts;
technology and manufacturing districts;

infrastructure megaprojects; and

anticipated private-investor returns.

The potential functional connection is straightforward:
destruction and displacement remove civilians from established communities;
concentrated aid sites influence where survivors travel;
identity and biometric systems register the remaining population;
transition areas consolidate displaced residents;
relocation models calculate the cost of departure;
digital tokens convert property rights into administrable instruments;
redevelopment entities obtain control of land; and
private contractors and investors participate in reconstruction.

This is an investigative hypothesis supported by documented components. Proof of a unified scheme requires communications, common personnel, data transfers, funding records, shared maps, and evidence that humanitarian operations were designed with the redevelopment objective in mind.

XV. DOGE and the removal of independent oversight

DOGE’s principal documented connection is the degradation of USAID’s capacity shortly before the GHF system became operational.

The USAID Office of Inspector General reported in February 2025 that the foreign-assistance pause and widespread staffing reductions had:

made important oversight functions largely nonoperational;
impaired partner vetting;
reduced the agency’s ability to prevent diversion to terrorist organizations;
interfered with monitoring of humanitarian commodities;
created uncertainty among implementing organizations; and
placed billions of dollars of obligated humanitarian assistance under weakened supervision.
USAID OIG oversight alert

The administration then removed USAID Inspector General Paul Martin shortly after his office issued that warning.

The resulting sequence deserves investigation:

established USAID operations were frozen or dismantled;

experienced personnel lost access or left the agency;

independent oversight capacity was degraded;

the Inspector General was removed;

the administration supported a new private Gaza aid system;
private contractors assumed logistics and security functions; and
the new system operated with limited transparency concerning funding, ownership, data, and accountability.
These facts establish weakened oversight and institutional substitution. They do not alone prove that DOGE dismantled USAID specifically to create the GHF system.
XVI. Counterterrorism justification and its contradiction
GHF and supporting authorities argued that a new distribution system was needed to prevent Hamas from diverting humanitarian assistance.
Preventing terrorist diversion is a legitimate governmental objective. But the USAID Inspector General warned that DOGE-related staffing reductions themselves weakened partner vetting and controls designed to prevent assistance from reaching sanctioned or violent organizations.
The policy therefore contained an apparent contradiction:

The government weakened an established counter-diversion and humanitarian-oversight institution while justifying a less transparent private replacement as necessary to prevent diversion.

Investigators must compare the actual safeguards of both systems:

pre-award vetting;

beneficial-ownership checks;

sanctions screening;

inventory reconciliation;

recipient verification;

loss and diversion reporting;

independent audits;

whistleblower channels;

procurement competition;

use-of-force reporting;

data protection; and

inspector-general access.

A counterterrorism label cannot substitute for audited controls.
XVII. Funding and financial accountability
The complete sources and uses of GHF funds were not publicly transparent during its operations.
Investigators should determine:
which governments funded GHF directly or indirectly;
whether funds moved through grants, intelligence budgets, defense accounts, private donors, or intermediaries;

the amount paid to Safe Reach Solutions and UG Solutions;

contractor profit margins;

payments to consultants and advisers;

insurance and indemnification arrangements;
whether subcontractors were competitively selected;
whether payments entered offshore accounts;
whether politically connected investors possessed economic interests;
whether expenses were later reimbursed by the United States; and
whether any GHF-derived infrastructure or information entered a commercial redevelopment project.
Because GHF publicly presented itself as a humanitarian nonprofit while relying on for-profit contractors, investigators must trace the ultimate recipient of every dollar.

XVIII. Closure of GHF does not eliminate accountability

GHF ceased distribution after the October 2025 ceasefire and announced in November 2025 that it was permanently ending operations. It claimed to have distributed more than 187 million meals and characterized its model as successful.

GHF’s closure does not resolve:

casualty responsibility;

data retention;

biometric records;

contractor profits;

weapons-discharge incidents;

ownership arrangements;
communications with governmental authorities;
connections to relocation modeling;
records transferred to successor organizations; or
whether its operational model survived through the United States-led Civil-Military Coordination Center.
Associated Press report on GHF’s closure
Every operational and financial record should have been preserved before dissolution, contract termination, device return, cloud-account closure, or contractor offboarding.
XIX. Applicable legal issues
Depending on the evidence, relevant authorities may include:
the Fourth Geneva Convention’s protections against forcible transfer;
customary international humanitarian law;
the Genocide Convention;

crimes-against-humanity principles;

federal material-support statutes;

sanctions and anti-money-laundering laws;

federal procurement-integrity requirements;

the False Claims Act;

wire and mail fraud statutes;
federal records and obstruction statutes;
privacy and data-protection requirements;
restrictions governing human experimentation;
contract use-of-force and incident-reporting provisions;
state wrongful-death and negligence law; and
corporate aiding-and-abetting or conspiracy principles where recognized.
Application of United States criminal statutes to overseas conduct requires careful analysis of extraterritorial reach, nationality, jurisdiction, statutory definitions, and the conduct of each defendant.
XX. RICO relevance
Operating a private aid organization, using security contractors, performing identity verification, or preparing relocation scenarios does not independently constitute racketeering.
RICO relevance could arise if the evidence demonstrates an enterprise conducting a pattern of qualifying predicates, potentially including:

wire fraud through false representations concerning neutrality, funding, safety, biometric use, or contractor ownership;

fraudulent inducement of civilians to surrender property or relocation rights;

material support knowingly provided to designated terrorist organizations;

money laundering or sanctions evasion;

bribery connected to humanitarian or reconstruction contracts;

obstruction through destruction or concealment of operational records;
retaliation against whistleblowers;
falsification of casualty or distribution reports;
theft or conversion of aid;
trafficking-related conduct; or
laundering proceeds obtained through an independently unlawful displacement scheme.
International-law violations and humanitarian-policy failures are not automatically RICO predicates. Each alleged predicate must be tied to a person, communication, transaction, victim, jurisdiction, and resulting injury.

